`setBackend("webgl")` throws immediately ("not found in registry") — so initTf is NOT the hang point... The catch calls `await tf.setBackend("cpu")` — 'cpu' should be registered by tfjs (required by tfjs-node). Hmm — is 'cpu' registered? tfjs-node requires '@tensorflow/tfjs' which registers cpu+webgl... apparently NOT webgl. Does it register cpu? Earlier regression test used... wait, in loadHW5 with tfjs-node, I call `await tf.ready()` — backend = 'tensorflow' — never touched cpu. If 'cpu' is NOT registered, `tf.setBackend("cpu")` THROWS → uncaught inside initTf?? Look at initTf:

```js
try {
  await tf.setBackend("webgl");
} catch (error) {
  await tf.setBackend("cpu");
}
await tf.ready();
```
If setBackend("cpu") throws (backend not found), the error propagates out of initTf → window.onload: `const backend = await initTf(); if (!backend) return;` — the throw escapes window.onload → my `await windowStub.onload()` rejections → caught by main().catch → "Fatal:" printed... but I saw NO output at all — not even the "tf module:" line. Hmm wait — the "tf module" line prints BEFORE onload. With grep buffering, ALL output was buffered and lost at timeout... no wait, on timeout the shell kills the process; grep flushes its buffer as it received data... Actually when the parent pipeline is killed (timeout), grep receives EOF and would flush what it had — but the output said "(no output)". Hmm, if `node` was killed first, grep would flush and print. Unless the pipeline kill killed grep before flush. Either way, can't conclude.

Actually — wait. Let me reconsider: if 'cpu' IS registered (it should be — the pure-JS cpu backend comes with @tensorflow/tfjs core bundle which tfjs-node depends on...). Let me verify quickly: `require('@tensorflow/tfjs-node').setBackend('cpu')`.

And also verify what happens on the full onload with direct (unbuffered) output and a hard timeout. Let me test setBackend('cpu') in tfjs-node.

