The hang is in the **Train click** — i.e., `runPipeline()` with the **cpu** backend (initTf switched from tensorflow→cpu in Node!).20 epochs × 1250 batches on the pure-JS CPU backend ≈ 25,000 batches × ~100ms = ~40 minutes. That's why it "hangs" — it's just extremely slow, and my4-10 min timeouts killed it mid-training. The earlier fast runs used the native 'tensorflow' backend (12.3s).

Note: this also mirrors the real browser situation IF WebGL is unavailable — the page would fall back to cpu and take ~40 min. In the browser, WebGL is likely available, so it's much faster. But my headless Node UI-flow must not take 40 minutes.

Fix for the test harness: in ui-flow.test.js, after `windowStub.onload()` completes initTf (which set cpu), switch the backend BACK to the native 'tensorflow' backend before clicking Train: `await tf.setBackend("tensorflow")`. The page code itself doesn't care which backend runs the ops. That keeps the bootstrap path authentic (initTf still exercised) while making training fast.

This is a test-environment concern, not an app defect: on a real browser WebGL will be selected by initTf. I'll note it.

Also — important insight for the app: with the CPU fallback, 20 epochs is very slow (but functional). Not a defect to fix (scaffolding chose the defaults); I'll mention it in the report.

Update ui-flow.test.js: after onload asserts, add:
```js
// initTf selected the pure-JS 'cpu' backend in Node (no WebGL); switch to the
// native tfjs-node backend for speed. The page code is backend-agnostic.
if (tf.getBackend() !== "tensorflow") {
  try { await tf.setBackend("tensorflow"); } catch (e) { /* keep current */ }
}
console.log(`backend for training: ${tf.getBackend()}`);
```
main() is async ✓.

Also lower risk: the recall click later also runs on whatever backend. Fine.

